(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-master-drill"]={"title":"Master Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug causes body flushing, fever and chills during a rapid infusion — Red Man Syndrome?","choices":["Vancomycin","Linezolid","Clindamycin","Gentamicin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Red Man Syndrome, also called Vancomycin Infusion Syndrome. It is an infusion-rate reaction, not an allergy, so the answer is to slow the infusion. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which antifungal causes visual disturbance in about thirty per cent of patients?","choices":["Fluconazole","Voriconazole","Itraconazole","Posaconazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Visual effects in 30% — the standout adverse effect of this azole. Used for systemic Aspergillus and Candida. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which neuromuscular blocker causes malignant hyperthermia?","choices":["Rocuronium","Vecuronium","Succinylcholine","Atracurium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Malignant hyperthermia and hyperkalaemia. Treatment is rapid cooling and dantrolene. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which decongestant causes rebound congestion — rhinitis medicamentosa — if used for more than three days?","choices":["Pseudoephedrine","Phenylephrine","Albuterol","Oxymetazoline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rhinitis medicamentosa beyond THREE DAYS. That three-day limit is the patient-education point. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which acne treatment bleaches hair and clothing?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide","Azelaic acid","Tretinoin","Clindamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It bleaches hair and clothes — the practical warning every patient needs before the first application. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which class must be separated from antacids, iron and dairy because it chelates with cations?","choices":["Macrolides","Tetracyclines","Penicillins","Cephalosporins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They chelate with cations, so the dose has to be spaced away from antacids, iron and dairy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which antibiotic class causes tendonitis and Achilles tendon rupture?","choices":["Macrolides","Tetracyclines","Fluoroquinolones","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tendonitis and Achilles rupture, with caution under 18 years old. Peripheral neuropathy belongs to the same class. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which antifungal's infusion causes fever and chills severe enough to limit treatment?","choices":["Fluconazole","Caspofungin","Griseofulvin","Amphotericin B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever and chills, hypotension, and hypokalaemia with hypomagnesaemia — side effects that limit treatment. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which neuromuscular blocker releases histamine?","choices":["Atracurium","Succinylcholine","Rocuronium","Pancuronium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Histamine release is what separates it from the other nondepolarizing blockers. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which drug induces REFLEX BRADYCARDIA?","choices":["Isoproterenol","Phenylephrine","Dobutamine","Epinephrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pure alpha-1 vasoconstriction raises blood pressure, and the baroreceptor answer is a reflex slowing of the heart. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which topical acne agent can cause hypopigmentation?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide","Adapalene","Azelaic acid","Erythromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypopigmentation alongside mild irritation and dryness. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which class must be avoided in children under eight years old?","choices":["Macrolides","Cephalosporins","Penicillins","Tetracyclines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Under eight, because of tooth discolouration and depressed skeletal growth. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which antibiotic causes a disulfiram-like reaction if the patient drinks alcohol?","choices":["Metronidazole","Clindamycin","Vancomycin","Linezolid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol — the patient must be told not to drink. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which is the only azole effective against the Zygomycetes, Mucor and Rhizopus?","choices":["Fluconazole","Posaconazole","Voriconazole","Itraconazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The ONLY azole covering Zygomycetes. It also prolongs the QT interval and drops potassium and magnesium. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which drug reactivates acetylcholinesterase after organophosphate poisoning?","choices":["Atropine","Physostigmine","Pralidoxime","Neostigmine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It reactivates inhibited acetylcholinesterase. Two limits: it does not enter the central nervous system, and it cannot overcome reversible inhibitors such as physostigmine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Extravasation causing blanching and sloughing of the skin belongs to which drug?","choices":["Dopamine","Dobutamine","Isoproterenol","Norepinephrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Extravasation blanches then sloughs the skin, and it is treated with the alpha antagonist phentolamine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which oral acne drug causes retinoid dermatitis, photophobia and raised serum lipids?","choices":["Isotretinoin","Doxycycline","Spironolactone","Benzoyl peroxide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Retinoid dermatitis, photophobia, arthralgia, headache, alopecia, brittle nails and raised lipids. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which class is used with caution in patients under eighteen?","choices":["Macrolides","Fluoroquinolones","Cephalosporins","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Caution under 18, because of the tendon and cartilage effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which antibiotic can cause serotonin syndrome with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors?","choices":["Vancomycin","Metronidazole","Linezolid","Tigecycline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, and it also interacts with tyramine-containing foods and pseudoephedrine. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which antifungal penetrates the central nervous system and is used for cryptococcal meningitis?","choices":["Amphotericin B","Griseofulvin","Caspofungin","Fluconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Best oral absorption of the azoles and it crosses into the central nervous system. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which cholinesterase inhibitor is used to DIAGNOSE myasthenia gravis?","choices":["Edrophonium","Pyridostigmine","Neostigmine","Physostigmine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Very short acting, so it is used for diagnosis and to reverse nondepolarizing blockers after surgery. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which drug is given to prevent dermal necrosis after norepinephrine extravasation?","choices":["Phenoxybenzamine","Phentolamine","Propranolol","Prazosin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An alpha antagonist, infiltrated locally. It is also used for short-term management of phaeochromocytoma and for hypertensive crisis after abrupt clonidine withdrawal. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which class must be avoided in pregnancy and causes photosensitivity and severe sunburn?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide","Azelaic acid","Topical retinoids","Bacitracin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tretinoin, adapalene and tazarotene — photosensitivity, severe sunburn, and avoided in pregnancy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Dizziness, insomnia and somnolence — central nervous system toxicity — belong to which class?","choices":["Tetracyclines","Cephalosporins","Polymyxins","Fluoroquinolones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Central toxicity sits alongside the tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Seizures are the warning attached to which antibiotic class, and to imipenem in particular?","choices":["Carbapenems","Cephalosporins","Monobactams","Penicillins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Seizures, specifically with imipenem. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which antifungal is teratogenic?","choices":["Fluconazole","Itraconazole","Terbinafine","Nystatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Teratogenic — the flag attached to this azole specifically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which cholinesterase inhibitor is used for the CHRONIC management of myasthenia gravis?","choices":["Edrophonium","Physostigmine","Pyridostigmine","Pralidoxime"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Long acting, so it is the maintenance drug — edrophonium is the diagnostic one. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"“First-dose” syncope is the warning attached to which class?","choices":["Beta-1 selective blockers","Beta-2 agonists","Antimuscarinics","Alpha-1 blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. First-dose syncope with orthostatic hypotension — which is why the first dose is taken at bedtime. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"The erythema, desquamation, burning and stinging from topical retinoids do what over time?","choices":["Decrease with time and with emollients","Worsen steadily until the drug is stopped","Are unaffected by emollients","Indicate an allergic reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They settle — which is exactly what the patient must be told, or they stop the drug in week two. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Gastrointestinal upset that is worst with erythromycin, especially in children and young adults, points to which class?","choices":["Penicillins","Macrolides","Tetracyclines","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nausea, diarrhoea and abdominal pain, worse with erythromycin than with the newer members. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which class discolours the teeth and depresses skeletal growth?","choices":["Macrolides","Fluoroquinolones","Tetracyclines","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tooth discolouration, doxycycline more than the others, and depression of skeletal growth. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which antifungal inhibits CYP3A4 and so carries significant drug interactions?","choices":["Griseofulvin","Nystatin","Caspofungin","Ketoconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A CYP3A4 inhibitor, which is why its interaction list is the longest of the azoles. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which agent stimulates a postpartum or postoperative atonic bladder and has NO nicotinic activity?","choices":["Bethanechol","Carbachol","Pilocarpine","Neostigmine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Muscarinic only, with no nicotinic action, so it is used for the atonic bladder. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which alpha-1 blockers are used specifically for benign prostatic hyperplasia?","choices":["Prazosin","Tamsulosin","Phentolamine","Labetalol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The uroselective ones — the rest of the class is used for blood pressure. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic is preferred for acne, though it is losing efficacy to resistance?","choices":["Erythromycin","Mupirocin","Clindamycin","Bacitracin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The preferred topical antibiotic, with efficacy falling over time because of Propionibacterium acnes resistance. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Interstitial nephritis with haematuria and crystalluria belongs to which class?","choices":["Macrolides","Aminoglycosides","Fluoroquinolones","Aminopenicillins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alongside hepatic dysfunction, Clostridium difficile infection and the hypersensitivity spectrum. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is the one classically named for Clostridium difficile and pseudomembranous colitis?","choices":["Clindamycin","Aztreonam","Linezolid","Vancomycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clostridium difficile and pseudomembranous colitis, plus rash, neutropenia and thrombocytopenia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which antifungal induces CYP1A2 and CYP2C9 and is absorbed better with a high-fat meal?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Griseofulvin","Fluconazole","Terbinafine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An inducer, taken with a fatty meal, and it causes headache in about 15% with blurred vision. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which cholinergic agonist has both muscarinic AND nicotinic activity and is used to lower intraocular pressure?","choices":["Bethanechol","Pilocarpine","Carbachol","Atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both receptor types, and it decreases intraocular pressure in glaucoma. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which beta blocker causes arrhythmias if it is stopped abruptly?","choices":["Phentolamine","Prazosin","Albuterol","Propranolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Abrupt withdrawal causes rebound arrhythmia — the class must be tapered. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which drugs are used as antiandrogens for acne in some women?","choices":["Spironolactone","Isotretinoin","Doxycycline","Adapalene"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Useful in some women, working on the hormonal driver rather than the follicle. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which class is mostly renally eliminated, so its dosing changes with kidney function?","choices":["Macrolides","Cephalosporins","Allylamines","Topical corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mostly renally eliminated — which is why the interval is stretched in renal impairment. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which class prolongs the QT interval and risks Torsades de pointes, worse with class Ia and III antiarrhythmics?","choices":["Penicillins","Aminoglycosides","Macrolides","Carbapenems"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. QT prolongation and Torsades de pointes, and the combination with class Ia and III antiarrhythmics makes it worse. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which antifungal is combined with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis and is hepatotoxic?","choices":["Fluconazole","Voriconazole","Micafungin","Flucytosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Given with amphotericin B in cryptococcal meningitis; hepatotoxicity is its flag. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which drug lowers intraocular pressure AND stimulates salivation in dry mouth?","choices":["Pilocarpine","Carbachol","Bethanechol","Timolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both indications belong to it — glaucoma and xerostomia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Fasting hypoglycaemia with raised low-density lipoprotein and triglycerides belongs to which drug?","choices":["Atenolol","Propranolol","Clonidine","Prazosin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic effects of nonselective beta blockade, alongside sexual impairment and central effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Local tissue atrophy and adrenal suppression, with the need to taper to prevent a flare, belong to which acne treatment?","choices":["Topical retinoids","Benzoyl peroxide","Corticosteroids","Azelaic acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrophy locally, adrenal suppression systemically, and tapering to prevent rebound. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Tachycardia, headache, insomnia, low potassium and low magnesium describe which antifungal class?","choices":["Azoles","Polyenes","Allylamines","Echinocandins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin, which also cause blood dyscrasias. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Renal toxicity and ototoxicity together point to which class?","choices":["Aminoglycosides","Macrolides","Cephalosporins","Clindamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both at once — the kidney and the eighth nerve. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which class is used for oesophageal candidiasis and for aspergillosis that has failed itraconazole or amphotericin?","choices":["Polyenes","Echinocandins","Allylamines","Griseofulvin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin — with tachycardia, headache, insomnia and low potassium and magnesium. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Dry mouth, blurred vision and “sandy eyes” describe the effects of which drug?","choices":["Bethanechol","Pilocarpine","Atropine","Neostigmine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Classic antimuscarinic effects, along with urinary retention and constipation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which drug causes REBOUND HYPERTENSION if stopped abruptly?","choices":["Propranolol","Prazosin","Tamsulosin","Clonidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rebound hypertension on abrupt discontinuation — and phentolamine is used if a crisis follows. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Skin atrophy, acne, rosacea and allergic dermatitis are the LOCAL effects of which class?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Topical retinoids","Topical immunomodulators","Topical antifungals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The local list. Systemically they can suppress the adrenal axis, raise glucose, and cause glaucoma, cataracts and growth retardation in children. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which antifungal class is used in the febrile neutropenic patient who is not responding to antibiotics?","choices":["Allylamines","Echinocandins","Topical azoles","Topical antifungals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Given when antibiotics have not worked, and for aspergillosis that has failed itraconazole or amphotericin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome with thrombocytopenia and agranulocytosis is the warning on which agent?","choices":["Amoxicillin / clavulanate","Piperacillin / tazobactam","Trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole","Nitrofurantoin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypersensitivity through to Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, plus blood dyscrasias. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which topical antifungal has NO activity against Candida?","choices":["Nystatin","Clotrimazole","Miconazole","Tolnaftate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tolnaftate has no candidal activity — nystatin is the one for candidal infection. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"“Mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet” describes toxicity from which group?","choices":["Antimuscarinics","Cholinergic agonists","Beta blockers","Organophosphates"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The anticholinergic toxidrome. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which drug is used to minimise withdrawal from opiates, tobacco and benzodiazepines?","choices":["Propranolol","Clonidine","Phenoxybenzamine","Amphetamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A central alpha-2 agonist, used to blunt withdrawal. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Glaucoma, cataracts and growth retardation in children are SYSTEMIC risks of which class?","choices":["Topical antifungals","Benzoyl peroxide","Topical corticosteroids","Mupirocin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Absorbed steroid produces systemic effects — which is why potency and duration are limited, especially in children. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which class treats athlete's foot, ringworm, jock itch and onychomycosis as well as the systemic mycoses?","choices":["Echinocandins","Polyenes","Topical corticosteroids","Azoles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The breadth is the point: superficial tinea through to Candida, Cryptococcus, Blastomyces, Histoplasma, Coccidioides and Aspergillus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which class carries black box warnings for nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity and neuromuscular blockade?","choices":["Polymyxins","Aminoglycosides","Vancomycin","Tigecycline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymyxin B and E (colistin) — all three warnings, and use is rising because of multidrug resistance. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which antiviral crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration must be maintained?","choices":["Oseltamivir","Acyclovir","Ganciclovir","Famciclovir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Crystallises in the renal tubule; it also causes seizures, delirium and tremor centrally. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which drug shows bradycardia at lower doses but tachycardia at higher doses?","choices":["Propranolol","Bethanechol","Atropine","Isoproterenol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The dose-dependent reversal that catches people out. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is given intravenously for a hypertensive emergency?","choices":["Propranolol","Atenolol","Timolol","Labetalol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous labetalol for hypertensive emergencies; it and carvedilol both cause orthostatic hypotension through alpha-1 blockade. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which agents are second-line for atopic dermatitis, after topical steroids, and cause a burning sensation?","choices":["Tacrolimus","Topical corticosteroids","Topical retinoids","Imiquimod"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical immunomodulators, second line, with burning on application — and avoided in patients with weakened immune systems. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Use of which class is expected to rise because of increasing multidrug resistance?","choices":["Penicillins","Polymyxins","Macrolides","Tetracyclines"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Old, toxic, and coming back because gram-negative resistance is leaving fewer options. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which antibiotic can be given to a genuinely penicillin-allergic patient because it has no cross-reactivity with beta-lactams?","choices":["Cefazolin","Meropenem","Aztreonam","Ampicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. No cross-reactivity with the beta-lactams, so it is usable in true penicillin allergy. Gram-negative cover only. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which antiviral causes neutropenia in fifteen to forty per cent of patients?","choices":["Acyclovir","Oseltamivir","Valacyclovir","Ganciclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neutropenia in 15-40%, with central effects in 5-15%; a third of patients must stop intravenous treatment because of side effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinic is used to prevent motion sickness, with instructions to wash the hands after applying it?","choices":["Scopolamine","Atropine","Ipratropium","Oxybutynin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A patch for motion sickness — and hands must be washed, or the drug is transferred to the eye. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which beta blocker prevents cardiovascular mortality in heart failure?","choices":["Propranolol","Carvedilol","Esmolol","Timolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Named for the mortality benefit in heart failure. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which topical class must be avoided in patients with weakened immune systems?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Topical antifungals","Topical immunomodulators","Benzoyl peroxide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tacrolimus and pimecrolimus suppress local immunity, so they are avoided where immunity is already impaired. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which class covers gram-negative organisms only?","choices":["Macrolides","Cephalosporins","Allylamines","Polymyxins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gram-negative only, which is why they are reserved for resistant gram-negative infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which penicillin group was designed solely to cover methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus?","choices":["Nafcillin","Trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole","Ampicillin","Piperacillin/tazobactam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Designed solely for MSSA — and monitored for hepatic rather than renal toxicity, the exception among the beta-lactams. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which antiviral is used for influenza A and B and requires renal dose adjustment?","choices":["Acyclovir","Oseltamivir","Ganciclovir","Famciclovir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A neuraminidase inhibitor for influenza A and B, with renal dose adjustment. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which inhaled antimuscarinics are used for maintenance bronchodilation in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Albuterol","Scopolamine","Tiotropium","Atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Maintenance inhaled bronchodilators; glycopyrrolate in the same group is also used for drooling and hyperhidrosis. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which beta blocker reduces production of aqueous humour in chronic open-angle glaucoma?","choices":["Propranolol","Carvedilol","Atenolol","Timolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It reduces aqueous production — a different mechanism from pilocarpine, which improves outflow. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic has no systemic toxicity and only rarely causes allergic dermatitis?","choices":["Bacitracin","Neomycin","Polymyxin B","Mupirocin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rare allergic dermatitis, no systemic toxicity. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Anxiety, tremor and headache, with poor penetration into the central nervous system, describe which class?","choices":["Alpha-1 blockers","Catecholamines","Beta-1 selective blockers","Antimuscarinics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, isoproterenol and dopamine — peripheral effects dominate because they do not cross well. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for syphilis, gas gangrene and meningococcus?","choices":["Doxycycline","Azithromycin","Penicillin G","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for all three. Good against gram-positives, with no staphylococcal cover. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In chickenpox, giving an antiviral within the first twenty-four hours does what?","choices":["Cures the infection completely","Prevents post-herpetic neuralgia entirely","Has no effect on the course at all","Shortens the illness but does not cure it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Within the first 24 hours it shortens the acute illness — and it does not clear the latent virus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinics lower intravesical pressure and increase bladder capacity?","choices":["Oxybutynin","Bethanechol","Ipratropium","Scopolamine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The bladder antimuscarinics, reducing the frequency of bladder contractions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Cardioselectivity is LOST under what circumstance?","choices":["In renal impairment","At higher doses","When given intravenously","In elderly patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta-1 selective agents stop being selective as the dose rises, which matters in a patient with airway disease. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"Which tetracycline restriction matters most when prescribing for skin disease?","choices":["Contraindicated in renal failure and dialysis","Contraindicated with dairy and antacids only","Contraindicated under 8 years old and in pregnancy","Contraindicated over 65 years of age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Contraindicated in children under eight and in pregnant women — the same rule that governs systemic use. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which class is used during orthopaedic surgery for fracture alignment and dislocation correction?","choices":["Cholinergic agonists","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Antimuscarinics","Nondepolarizing blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adjuvants in anaesthesia — intubation, and relaxing muscle enough to reduce a fracture or dislocation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for neutropenic fever?","choices":["Cefepime","Cefazolin","Ceftriaxone","Cephalexin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The fourth-generation cephalosporin, drug of choice for neutropenic fever. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which agents are used for hookworms, roundworms, pinworms and whipworms?","choices":["Pyrantel pamoate","Albendazole","Metronidazole","Ivermectin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The benzimidazoles, and they are very well tolerated. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which cholinesterase inhibitor crosses into the central nervous system and can cause convulsions at high dose?","choices":["Neostigmine","Pyridostigmine","Physostigmine","Pralidoxime"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It enters the central nervous system, which is why it is the one used for anticholinergic toxicity — and why it convulses at high dose. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which class is useful for hypertension in a patient with impaired pulmonary function?","choices":["Nonselective beta blockers","Cholinergic agonists","Alpha-1 blockers","Beta-1 selective blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atenolol, metoprolol, bisoprolol and esmolol — selective, so the airway is spared. But only at lower doses. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"Which acne agent is used when resistance has made a topical antibiotic less useful, and is often combined with it?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide","Isotretinoin","Spironolactone","Tacrolimus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Combining it with a topical antibiotic slows the resistance that is eroding clindamycin and erythromycin. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Poisoning by which group produces EITHER nicotinic OR muscarinic signs?","choices":["Alpha-1 blockers","Organophosphates","Beta blockers","Nondepolarizing blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Either picture can dominate, which is why the presentation varies so much. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin cannot be used during the first thirty days of life?","choices":["Cefotaxime","Cefazolin","Ceftriaxone","Cefepime"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Not in the first 30 days of life; cefotaxime is preferred for neonatal fever or sepsis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which drug is used to eliminate nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus, especially MRSA?","choices":["Bacitracin","Neomycin","Polymyxin B","Mupirocin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Applied intranasally to clear carriage, MRSA included. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which drugs slow the progression of Alzheimer's disease and mostly cause gastrointestinal distress?","choices":["Donepezil","Neostigmine","Edrophonium","Atropine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The centrally acting cholinesterase inhibitors, whose limiting side effect is gastrointestinal. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which drug is used for cardiogenic and septic shock, acting as a beta-1 inotrope with alpha-1 vasoconstriction only at very high doses?","choices":["Dobutamine","Dopamine","Phenylephrine","Isoproterenol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The dose-response is the whole point: beta-1 first, alpha-1 only at the top of the range. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which agent causes skin irritation in virtually every patient who uses it?","choices":["Bacitracin","Mupirocin","Imiquimod","Nystatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Irritation in virtually all patients — expected, and not a reason to stop. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which vehicle makes a topical corticosteroid MOST potent?","choices":["Cream","Lotion","Gel","Ointment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ointment beats cream, which beats lotion — the vehicle changes the potency, not just the feel. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Penicillin cross-sensitivity of roughly less than one per cent belongs to which class?","choices":["Cephalosporins","Carbapenems","Monobactams","Macrolides"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cross-sensitivity of approximately less than 1%, and mostly renally eliminated. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic frequently causes sensitisation?","choices":["Mupirocin","Neomycin","Bacitracin","Clindamycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Frequently sensitises, and it can accumulate systemically. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Salivation, flushing, falling blood pressure, abdominal pain, diarrhoea and bronchospasm together are the effects of what?","choices":["Anticholinergic excess","Beta blockade","Cholinergic excess","Alpha-1 blockade"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The muscarinic picture produced by cholinesterase inhibitors and direct agonists. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which drug increases atrioventricular conduction, so it needs caution in atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Phenylephrine","Clonidine","Propranolol","Dobutamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Increased AV conduction is the caution — in atrial fibrillation it can speed the ventricular response. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which topical antifungal is for candidal infection specifically?","choices":["Nystatin","Tolnaftate","Terbinafine","Ciclopirox"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For candidal infection — tolnaftate in the same pairing has NO candidal activity. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which agent inactivates tretinoin if the two are applied together?","choices":["Clindamycin","Benzoyl peroxide","Azelaic acid","Tacrolimus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Benzoyl peroxide inactivates tretinoin, so they are applied at different times of day. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which agent significantly raises the international normalised ratio in a patient on warfarin, and causes hypoglycaemia with sulfonylureas?","choices":["Amoxicillin / clavulanate","Piperacillin / tazobactam","Trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole","Clindamycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both interactions belong to it, which is why the warfarin patient needs closer monitoring. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which topical agent must be kept off open or denuded skin in high doses?","choices":["Bacitracin","Mupirocin","Nystatin","Polymyxin B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Avoided on open wounds or denuded skin at high dose, because of neurotoxicity and nephrotoxicity. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which class must be used with caution in reactive airway disease because it causes bronchospasm?","choices":["Cholinergic agonists","Antimuscarinics","Beta-2 agonists","Alpha-1 blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bronchospasm is the reason for caution in reactive airway disease. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Cerebral haemorrhage from a sharp rise in blood pressure is a risk with which drug?","choices":["Albuterol","Epinephrine","Dobutamine","Clonidine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alongside anxiety, tremor, headache, arrhythmias and pulmonary oedema. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which topical drug is applied as a lacquer to the nail?","choices":["Terbinafine","Nystatin","Ciclopirox","Mupirocin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Penlac nail lacquer, for onychomycosis. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which class is losing efficacy over time because of Propionibacterium acnes resistance?","choices":["Topical retinoids","Topical corticosteroids","Topical immunomodulators","Topical antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Which is why they are paired with benzoyl peroxide rather than used alone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which class must be avoided in the second and third trimesters of pregnancy?","choices":["Tetracyclines","Penicillins","Cephalosporins","Macrolides"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Avoided in the second and third trimesters, and in children, because of tooth discolouration and skeletal effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which topical antifungal comes as a nail lacquer for onychomycosis?","choices":["Nystatin","Ciclopirox","Tolnaftate","Clotrimazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The nail lacquer formulation, applied to the nail plate. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Organophosphates are most commonly encountered as what?","choices":["Prescription eye drops","Topical acne treatments","Agricultural insecticides","Inhaled anaesthetics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Irreversible cholinesterase inhibitors used as insecticides; poisoning shows nicotinic or muscarinic signs. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which drug is used to stimulate the heart in an emergency and is a pure beta agonist?","choices":["Phenylephrine","Norepinephrine","Clonidine","Isoproterenol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nonselective beta, so it drives rate and contractility with no alpha vasoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which class treats athlete's foot, ringworm and jock itch — the superficial dermatophytes?","choices":["Allylamines","Nystatin","Bacitracin","Imiquimod"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Terbinafine and naftifine, oral or topical. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which class produces salivation, flushing, falling blood pressure, abdominal pain, diarrhoea and bronchospasm?","choices":["Nondepolarizing blockers","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Alpha-1 blockers","Antimuscarinics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The muscarinic picture from raising acetylcholine at every synapse. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Transient hearing loss and cholestatic hepatitis belong to which class?","choices":["Aminoglycosides","Tetracyclines","Macrolides","Fluoroquinolones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both, alongside the gastrointestinal upset that is worst with erythromycin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which topical class is first-line for superficial dermatophyte infection — athlete's foot, ringworm and jock itch?","choices":["Nystatin","Mupirocin","Bacitracin","Terbinafine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Terbinafine, oral or topical, for the superficial dermatophytes. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which drug is given as an ANTIDOTE to competitive neuromuscular blockers after surgery?","choices":["Neostigmine","Succinylcholine","Atropine","Pralidoxime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Raises acetylcholine to out-compete the blocker at the junction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Tremor, restlessness, apprehension and anxiety are the side effects of which drug?","choices":["Ipratropium","Albuterol","Propranolol","Atropine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta-2 agonist effects, and the reason a patient feels shaky after a rescue inhaler. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic can accumulate systemically if used over a large area?","choices":["Bacitracin","Mupirocin","Neomycin","Clindamycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Systemic accumulation, on top of frequent sensitisation. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"“Dry as a bone, blind as a bat, red as a beet, mad as a hatter” describes toxicity from which class?","choices":["Cholinesterase inhibitors","Catecholamines","Nondepolarizing blockers","Antimuscarinics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The anticholinergic toxidrome, each phrase naming one blocked muscarinic effect. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which agent does NOT cover vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus?","choices":["Tigecycline","Linezolid","Vancomycin","Clindamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Covers complicated skin and intra-abdominal infection but NOT VRE — the exception worth remembering. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which agent is used for external and perianal warts, actinic keratoses and basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Mupirocin","Imiquimod","Ciclopirox","Tacrolimus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All three indications, and it irritates the skin in virtually every patient — which is expected, not a reason to stop. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Nondepolarizing blockers are used for which purpose?","choices":["Treating myasthenia gravis","Reversing organophosphate poisoning","Relaxing skeletal muscle for intubation","Lowering intraocular pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adjuvants in anaesthesia — intubation, fracture alignment and dislocation correction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which alpha blocker is IRREVERSIBLE, so its effect lasts at least twenty-four hours?","choices":["Phentolamine","Prazosin","Tamsulosin","Phenoxybenzamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Irreversible blockade lasting at least 24 hours, used in phaeochromocytoma, and it produces epinephrine reversal. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which agents are used topically for recurrent cold sores?","choices":["Penciclovir","Imiquimod","Mupirocin","Tacrolimus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical antivirals for recurrent orolabial herpes simplex. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which class requires less frequent dosing as renal function falls?","choices":["Macrolides","Penicillins","Allylamines","Topical retinoids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Renally cleared, so the interval stretches as clearance drops. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for polymicrobial and nosocomial infections, especially pneumonia and intra-abdominal sepsis?","choices":["Amoxicillin/clavulanate","Cefazolin","Piperacillin/tazobactam","Aztreonam"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for polymicrobial and nosocomial infection, including hospital pneumonia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which topical agent is used for recurrent orolabial herpes simplex?","choices":["Imiquimod","Mupirocin","Bacitracin","Penciclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The topical antivirals for cold sores. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Nicotine at higher doses does what to blood pressure and to gut and bladder activity?","choices":["Blood pressure falls and gut activity ceases","Blood pressure rises further and gut activity increases","Both are unchanged","Blood pressure rises while gut activity ceases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stimulation first, then paralysis of all ganglia — so the early effects reverse. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which drug is used for hyperactivity, narcolepsy and appetite control?","choices":["Clonidine","Amphetamine","Cocaine","Pseudoephedrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All three indications, through raised blood pressure, cardiac stimulation and central activity. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which topical antifungals are used for vulvovaginal candidiasis?","choices":["Tolnaftate","Terbinafine","Topical azoles","Bacitracin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clotrimazole, miconazole and sertaconazole, topically and vaginally. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Hepatic dysfunction with jaundice and raised transaminases belongs to which penicillin group?","choices":["Penicillinase-resistant penicillins","Cephalosporins","Carbapenems","Aminopenicillins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hepatic dysfunction, hepatitis and jaundice with raised AST, ALT, bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for skin and soft tissue infection, diabetic foot, and animal or human bites?","choices":["Amoxicillin/clavulanate","Piperacillin/tazobactam","Ceftriaxone","Metronidazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for exactly those, because the clavulanate covers the mouth flora in a bite. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which antifungal class covers Candida, Cryptococcus, Blastomyces, Histoplasma, Coccidioides and Aspergillus?","choices":["Echinocandins","Azoles","Allylamines","Polyenes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The class spectrum — which is why the individual azoles are then separated by their own quirks. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Hyperkalaemia after a neuromuscular blocker points to which agent?","choices":["Rocuronium","Cisatracurium","Succinylcholine","Pancuronium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkalaemia alongside malignant hyperthermia — the two dangers of the depolarizing blocker. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which sympathomimetic has a legitimate use as a LOCAL ANAESTHETIC?","choices":["Amphetamine","Ephedrine","Tyramine","Cocaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Its one therapeutic use. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What is the most important thing to tell a patient starting a topical retinoid about the sun?","choices":["It causes photosensitivity and severe sunburn","It protects against sunburn","It has no interaction with sun exposure","It should only be applied in the morning"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Photosensitivity and severe sunburn — so sun protection is part of the prescription. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which class is second line for atopic dermatitis, after topical steroids?","choices":["Topical retinoids","Topical immunomodulators","Topical antibiotics","Topical antifungals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tacrolimus and pimecrolimus, used when steroids are not appropriate to continue. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which class is the drug of choice for multidrug-resistant gram-negative and extended-spectrum beta-lactamase infections?","choices":["Aminopenicillins","First-generation cephalosporins","Carbapenems","Macrolides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for MDR gram-negatives and ESBL producers. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which antifungal accumulates its cyclodextrin vehicle in renal failure?","choices":["Fluconazole","Amphotericin B","Caspofungin","Voriconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cyclodextrin carrier accumulates when renal function is poor. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Malignant hyperthermia is treated with rapid cooling and which drug?","choices":["Dantrolene","Pralidoxime","Neostigmine","Atropine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dantrolene, with rapid cooling. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Ephedra-containing herbal products were banned by the Food and Drug Administration in which year?","choices":["1994","2004","2010","2016"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Banned in 2004; pseudoephedrine remains available for nasal and sinus congestion. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which topical antibacterial must be kept off open or denuded skin at high dose?","choices":["Bacitracin","Mupirocin","Polymyxin B","Clindamycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because of neurotoxicity and nephrotoxicity if enough is absorbed. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which class must be applied with a warning about severe sunburn?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Topical immunomodulators","Topical antibiotics","Topical retinoids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Photosensitivity and severe sunburn, which is why they are applied at night and paired with sun protection. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which antibiotic covers gram-negative organisms only?","choices":["Aztreonam","Vancomycin","Ceftaroline","Piperacillin/tazobactam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gram-negative only — and no cross-reactivity with the other beta-lactams. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which drug is used with amphotericin B specifically because using it alone breeds resistance?","choices":["Fluconazole","Flucytosine","Voriconazole","Griseofulvin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Paired with amphotericin B in cryptococcal meningitis rather than used alone. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which agent has NO therapeutic use but causes a serious pressor reaction in a patient on a monoamine oxidase inhibitor?","choices":["Nicotine","Cocaine","Tyramine","Ephedrine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. No therapeutic use; found in fermented foods such as cheese and wine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Poor penetration into the central nervous system is a property of which class?","choices":["Amphetamines","Alpha-1 blockers","Antimuscarinics","Catecholamines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, isoproterenol and dopamine — they do not cross well, so their effects are peripheral. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which drug is applied inside the nose rather than to a lesion?","choices":["Mupirocin","Bacitracin","Imiquimod","Ciclopirox"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. To eliminate nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus, MRSA included. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which class causes bronchospasm and so must be used cautiously in reactive airway disease?","choices":["Antimuscarinics","Cholinergic agonists","Beta-2 agonists","Alpha-1 blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bronchospasm is the reason for caution — the airway constricts as secretions rise. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone must not be used for a urinary tract infection and has no Pseudomonas cover?","choices":["Levofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Moxifloxacin","Ofloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Not for urinary tract infection, and no Pseudomonas cover — levofloxacin is the one with it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia during treatment point to which antifungal?","choices":["Fluconazole","Terbinafine","Nystatin","Amphotericin B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Electrolyte wasting is part of its toxicity, alongside the infusion fever and hypotension. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which cholinergic agent is used to decrease intraocular pressure in glaucoma AND has both receptor actions, given intraocularly during surgery?","choices":["Carbachol","Bethanechol","Physostigmine","Scopolamine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both muscarinic and nicotinic, used intraocularly. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which drug produces orthostatic hypotension because it blocks alpha-1 in addition to beta receptors?","choices":["Atenolol","Labetalol","Propranolol","Timolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mixed alpha and beta blockade, which is where the orthostatic drop comes from. Carvedilol shares this. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which class is the drug of choice for febrile neutropenia, sepsis and enterococcal synergy?","choices":["Macrolides","Tetracyclines","Aminoglycosides","Clindamycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for all three, which is why the renal and ear monitoring matters. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which antiviral pair has actions and toxicity essentially the same as acyclovir?","choices":["Ganciclovir","Oseltamivir","Albendazole","Famciclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Explicitly described as similar to acyclovir in both action and toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Which effects would you expect from a patient who has taken too much of an antimuscarinic?","choices":["Confusion, hallucinations and delirium","Salivation, diarrhoea and bronchospasm","Bradycardia and hypotension","Tendon rupture and photosensitivity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The central antimuscarinic picture, on top of dry mouth, blurred vision and retention. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which agent is used for the SHORT-TERM management of phaeochromocytoma?","choices":["Phenoxybenzamine","Phentolamine","Propranolol","Clonidine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Short-term; phenoxybenzamine is the irreversible one used for longer preparation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which class causes photosensitivity and complexes with cations such as antacids and dairy?","choices":["Macrolides","Penicillins","Fluoroquinolones","Clindamycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Photosensitivity, and they chelate with cations — so they must be separated from antacids, iron and dairy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Seizures, delirium and tremor are the central nervous system effects of which antiviral?","choices":["Oseltamivir","Albendazole","Imiquimod","Acyclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Central effects alongside the renal crystallisation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which of these groups inhibits cholinesterase IRREVERSIBLY?","choices":["Organophosphates","Cholinergic agonists","Antimuscarinics","Nondepolarizing blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Irreversible, which is why the enzyme has to be reactivated with pralidoxime rather than simply out-competed. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which drug relieves nasal and sinus congestion and is taken by mouth?","choices":["Oxymetazoline","Pseudoephedrine","Albuterol","Isoproterenol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral decongestant — unlike oxymetazoline, which is the topical one with the three-day rebound limit. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, both to treat and to prevent it?","choices":["Clindamycin with primaquine","Azithromycin","Trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole","Fluconazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment AND prophylaxis, which is why it is used long term in immunosuppressed patients. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which topical antifungal is the one used for vulvovaginal candidiasis?","choices":["Tolnaftate","Terbinafine","Ciclopirox","Clotrimazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Topical and vaginal use for vulvovaginal candidiasis. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinic is used for excessive drooling in cerebral palsy and for hyperhidrosis?","choices":["Glycopyrrolate","Scopolamine","Oxybutynin","Atropine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Named for both, alongside its inhaled use in the same family. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Nausea, hypertension and arrhythmias are listed for which drug?","choices":["Albuterol","Dopamine","Clonidine","Prazosin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Its side effects, used in cardiogenic and septic shock. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin generation carries MRSA cover?","choices":["Cefazolin","Ceftriaxone","Ceftaroline","Cefepime"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The fifth generation, used for skin and soft tissue infection WITH MRSA cover — the only cephalosporin that has it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which antifungal is used for systemic aspergillosis and candidaemia and is given intravenously as an echinocandin?","choices":["Griseofulvin","Nystatin","Terbinafine","Caspofungin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An echinocandin, used when itraconazole or amphotericin has failed. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Why can pralidoxime not be used against physostigmine poisoning?","choices":["It cannot overcome reversible inhibitors","It is too slow to act","It is inactivated by the liver first","It only works in children"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It reactivates the enzyme after IRREVERSIBLE inhibition; a reversible inhibitor is not bound the same way. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Dizziness, lack of energy, nasal congestion and drowsiness point to which class?","choices":["Beta-2 agonists","Alpha-1 blockers","Antimuscarinics","Cholinergic agonists"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alpha-1 blockade opens the nasal vessels and drops the blood pressure — hence congestion plus dizziness. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"}]};
